b/ref 1033 46 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050373 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "43b84ada-660f-567c-bc31-48b5730b02b8" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124494 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124596 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124597 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1031 "Subspaces of Abstract Vector Spaces" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1033 1907 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050459 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4a436386-bf41-55c7-b1d3-02adad8f3faf" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049015 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119816 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119817 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119861 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1031 "The Column Space of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1033 1850 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186050662 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5ce2b050-dd59-54b3-bb85-3a4f08ea0365" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048343 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119932 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119933 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186119934 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1031 "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1033 1853 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186051052 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192510 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10975" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192510 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/72329ee334988361174013e7df57452851cd826acf2ffc8b4cead6d750683e73.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192510 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192511 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10976" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192511 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{ \\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 : x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{ \\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle : t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192511 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192511 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](images/6d/6da77166b57246e3d4bb076af75c54a10350678986adfb6e31125e0ef614bacc.svg) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](images/9c/9cfd2972828a5c45bc58b434debbcf4a96c2cab14daa36a1e07fc37011682960.svg) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](images/83/83ecb68263c21fca495034a2f1678dced07ddf9f5167c5a0a9c7703657530c47.svg)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192512 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10979" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192512 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c6/c66cdc8bbddfb00ce69f843b1ce1d12116b2dd82312a34abafa0fb13907110cf.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192512 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I  represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram II  represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin ![](images/45/4578110804bb0edbf2816e89b8231affe683257d5f37c1844294bb714f36a417.svg)  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051052 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193318 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186193389 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186247155 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275426 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317178 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323709 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407368 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407369 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186192786 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [1