gram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193657 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1061 41649 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93a1b04d-d02e-5aae-bd94-6c2a571d80d7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193653 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193882 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193654 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193658 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193654 #edb/ref 1061 41650 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193654 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193654 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192786 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193655 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193659 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193655 #edb/ref 1061 41653 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193655 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193655 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192787 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193656 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193655 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193656 #edb/ref 1061 41665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193656 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193656 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192788 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193654 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193657 #edb/ref 1061 41670 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193883 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193656 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193658 #edb/ref 1061 41676 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193884 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193659 #edb/ref 1061 41677 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193885 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193882 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193882 #edb/ref 1065 6854 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193882 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193882 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193883 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193883 #edb/ref 1065 6857 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193883 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193883 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193884 #edb/ref 1064 "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193884 #edb/ref 1065 6861 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193884 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193884 #edb/ref 1067 "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1064 "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1065 6862 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186193885 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273001 #edb/ref 1081 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273001 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273001 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "46f974ec-ee8c-52ea-81e3-8930b26a84ca" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273133 #edb/ref 1081 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273133 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273133 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "56b6f0bb-39ce-56d1-9d4c-0e806a85f6f6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273192 #edb/ref 1081 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273192 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186273192 #e