585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407356 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407357 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407358 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407359 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407361 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407358 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407367 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "6d1cab38-e87e-597f-87e0-47535946af67" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407366 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74214" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/42e0a9a9e987c1c56a335f29cf939e4f03d496fad148f148c32377893dbe7c13.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82f2815d-a760-5a7e-b3a4-18ea182996e3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407368 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.  \n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. There are vectors  $a=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$  in the subset for which ${a}_{1}>0$ and ${a}_{2}>0.$   But then\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1} \\\\ -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407367 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35574" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1d/1d5d9586684c8606ca0f6df8dd70d9828ea04d3d9097b5a155ab66bcd7300ffd.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "befb255b-f1bd-5c00-bc58-981c00e76ebb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407369 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case we also have that the vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"." #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
